        { "id": "prt_04b", "sessionID": "ses_06", "messageID": "msg_04", "type": "reasoning", "text": "The distinction the student is missing is what's real vs faked at the boundary, not how many internal functions get called.", "time": { "start": 1758720021000, "end": 1758720022000 } },
        { "id": "prt_04c", "sessionID": "ses_06", "messageID": "msg_04", "type": "text", "text": "Close, but it's about what's real at the boundary, not how many internal functions run. A unit test isolates one unit and fakes its collaborators (what you have). An integration test lets real collaborators talk to each other - real database, real socket layer - so you catch bugs that only show up when the pieces are actually wired together, like a schema mismatch or a wrong SQL column name. Calling three internal functions inside one mocked-out module is still a unit test of that module." },
